{#
  A new-password field, its live complexity checklist, and its confirmation
  — the whole thing, wired by nothing the calling page has to write.

  Every page that asks somebody to choose a password used to assemble these
  three pieces itself and hand-wire them in an inline <script>: the account
  page, the password-reset page, and — until this partial existed — the
  first-run setup page, which simply had none of it and accepted eight
  characters where the rest of the site requires twelve. Three copies of one
  rule is three chances to get it wrong, and the page that got it wrong was
  the one creating the SUPER-ADMIN account.

  Everything here is a convenience. `Core\Security\PasswordPolicy` is what
  actually decides, server-side, on every one of these forms; a checklist a
  visitor never sees changes nothing about what is accepted.

  Usage:
  {% include 'partials/password_field.html.twig' with {
      field_id: 'account-new-password',
      field_name: 'new_password',
      label: 'Nouveau mot de passe',
      confirm_name: 'confirm_password',   -- optional; omit for no confirmation
      required: true,                     -- default true
      help: '…',                          -- optional
      autocomplete: 'new-password',       -- default
  } %}

  The notes after `--` above are prose, not Twig. Twig comments do not
  nest, so a nested closing marker inside this block would END it, and
  everything below would render as markup — which is exactly how this
  partial first shipped a stray duplicate script tag onto every page that
  included it.

  The calling PAGE must once, anywhere on it, load
  assets/js/password-field.js as a MODULE — the file uses `export`, and a
  classic script tag rejects it with a SyntaxError before a line of it runs.

  `required: false` means an empty field is accepted and everything is
  skipped — the "leave blank to keep the current password" case. As soon as
  a character is typed the rules apply again.
#}
{% set _required = required is defined ? required : true %}
{% set _confirm_id = field_id ~ '-confirm' %}
<div class="password-field" data-password-field
     data-required="{{ _required ? '1' : '0' }}"
     data-input="{{ field_id }}"
     {% if confirm_name is defined and confirm_name %}data-confirm-field="{{ _confirm_id }}"{% endif %}>
    <div class="mb-2">
        <label class="form-label" for="{{ field_id }}">{{ label }}</label>
        <input type="password" class="form-control" id="{{ field_id }}" name="{{ field_name }}"
               {{ _required ? 'required' : '' }}
               autocomplete="{{ autocomplete|default('new-password') }}">
        {% if help is defined and help %}
            <div class="form-text">{{ help }}</div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    <div class="mb-2">
        {% include 'partials/password_complexity_checklist.html.twig' with {
            list_id: field_id ~ '-checklist',
        } %}
    </div>

    {% if confirm_name is defined and confirm_name %}
        <div class="mb-2">
            <label class="form-label" for="{{ _confirm_id }}">{{ confirm_label|default('Confirmer le mot de passe') }}</label>
            <input type="password" class="form-control" id="{{ _confirm_id }}" name="{{ confirm_name }}"
                   {{ _required ? 'required' : '' }} autocomplete="new-password">
        </div>
        <div class="text-danger small mb-2 d-none" data-password-mismatch>
            Les mots de passe ne correspondent pas.
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</div>
